1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,860
Same subgraph, same question, same model.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:06,022
Write it down another way,

3
00:00:06,022 --> 00:00:09,266
and accuracy drops from 41.8% to 13.6%.

4
00:00:09,266 --> 00:00:13,674
That 28-point gap has nothing to do with your prompt.

5
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:18,946
Four authors at Oracle laid out fifteen serialization formats,

6
00:00:18,946 --> 00:00:22,392
from semantic web triples down to adjacency lists,

7
00:00:22,392 --> 00:00:26,664
across nine frontier model configurations and four benchmarks.

8
00:00:26,664 --> 00:00:30,730
The control is clean. Only the serialization block changes.

9
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:34,460
Ontology extraction barely notices.

10
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:38,360
All nine configurations land between 45 and 55 F1,

11
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:42,182
and the cross-format spread is 1.3 to 3.9 points.

12
00:00:42,182 --> 00:00:44,834
That is not where the money hides.

13
00:00:45,834 --> 00:00:48,831
Structural reasoning is where the same choice

14
00:00:48,831 --> 00:00:50,229
is worth 28.4 points.

15
00:00:50,229 --> 00:00:53,493
Bigger graphs and harder operators widen the gap,

16
00:00:53,493 --> 00:00:55,890
and real subgraphs widen it further.

17
00:00:56,890 --> 00:01:01,473
One model family, three reasoning tiers, three favourite formats.

18
00:01:01,473 --> 00:01:04,788
The most accurate tier uses 1,008 input tokens,

19
00:01:04,788 --> 00:01:08,454
four and a half times cheaper than the default tier.

20
00:01:08,454 --> 00:01:11,698
The expensive model stops caring about format.

21
00:01:12,698 --> 00:01:14,383
Real graphs are worse.

22
00:01:14,383 --> 00:01:18,213
Strip the predicates and adjacency falls to 13.6%,

23
00:01:18,213 --> 00:01:21,659
while the most explicit format reaches 41.8%.

24
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:25,106
The answer sits in the part you stripped out.

25
00:01:26,106 --> 00:01:30,625
A per-question selector lifts 66.67% to 76.19%,

26
00:01:30,625 --> 00:01:32,932
for 3.71 times the cost.

27
00:01:32,932 --> 00:01:38,028
Majority voting spends 5.69 times and reaches 71.43%.

28
00:01:38,028 --> 00:01:41,778
And this holds inside one distribution.

29
00:01:42,778 --> 00:01:45,074
So go back to your own pipeline.

30
00:01:45,074 --> 00:01:48,947
Fix twenty questions and pick three to five spellings,

31
00:01:48,947 --> 00:01:51,028
then measure the spread once.

32
00:01:51,028 --> 00:01:55,762
Narrower than 3 points, pick one. Wider than 10, price a selector.
